>>> >> http://hlplab.wordpress.com/2010/05/10/mini-womm/
>>> >>
>>> >> I'm doing a mixed model logistic regression, with random intercepts
>>> >> for items and random slopes for items with respect to the fixed effect
>>> >> Indep2 (cf. slide 85):
>>> >>
>>> >> (a) glmer(formula = Dep ~ 1 + (1 | Item) + (0 + Indep2 | Item) +
>>> >> Indep1 + Indep2, data = my.data, family = binomial(link = "logit"))
>>> >>
>>> >> As per slide 88, I can also reduce the random effects to (1 + Indep2 |
>>> >> Item):
>>> >>
>>> >> (b) glmer(formula = Dep ~ 1 + (1 + Indep2 | Item) + Indep1 + Indep2,
>>> >> data = my.data, family = binomial(link = "logit"))
>>> >>
>>> >> It's not exactly clear to me what (1 + Indep2 | Item) does, since the
>>> >> output of both (a) and (b) includes random intercepts for items and
>>> >> random slopes for items by Indep2. At the same time, model (a) and (b)
>>> >> differ in their exact estimates.
>>> >>
>>> >> I would appreciate if someone could explain what the difference
>>> >> between model (a) and (b) is.
>>> >
>>> > (1 | Item) means that items are assumed to have different intercepts
>>> > -- like if "Dep" were some kind of error rate, then this would be
>>> > saying that some items are intrinsically more prone to errors
>>> > ("harder") and some are less prone to errors ("easier").
>>> >
>>> > (Indep2 | Item) means that some items are more or less strongly
>>> > affected by manipulations in Indep2.
>>> >
>>> > (1 | Item) + (Indep2 | Item) means that both of these things are
>>> > assumed to be true. However, it is assumed that these two kinds of
>>> > variation are *independent*. I.e., some hard items are strongly or
>>> > weakly affected by Indep2, and some easy items are strongly or weakly
>>> > affected by Indep2, but on average Indep2 effects hard items and easy
>>> > items equally.
>>> >
>>> > (1 + Indep2 | Item) means that both of these things are assumed to be
>>> > true, and also removes this independence assumption. It means that
>>> > there might be some correlation where e.g. items which are hard also
>>> > tend to be strongly affected by Indep2 while easy items tend to be
>>> > weakly affected.
>>> >
>>> > When you fit the model, you'll see the difference if you look at the
>>> > random effects correlation matrix. The (1 | Item) + (Indep2 | Item)
>>> > model will have two numbers, which are estimates of the per-item
>>> > variance in the intercept and in the Indep2 effect. The (1 + Indep2 |
>>> > Item) will have an extra number, which is the estimate of the
>>> > covariance between the intercept and the Indep2 effect. The (1 | Item)
>>> > + (Indep2 | Item) model is equivalent to forcing this third number to
>>> > be zero.
>>> >
>>> > -- Nathaniel
